From 874f40b8025b88248cf99affef8863b7c62966fa Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Will Norris <will@biohack.net>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 19:44:55 +0700
Subject: [PATCH 17/17] Recover near-index no-wrap proofs after loop strength
 reduction

On the 65816, indexed addressing carries into the next data bank, so a near pointer addition can fold into an index only when its unsigned sum cannot wrap. Loop strength reduction rebuilds byte addresses without their original inbounds flags. Copy and fill loops then form every address explicitly instead of using (zp),y or abs,x.

Immediately after LSR, record nuw on scalar address-space-0 byte GEPs with a single 16-bit index when ScalarEvolution shows that the result is at least the base: either the address is a nuw recurrence starting at that base, or the unsigned comparison is proven directly. This shape has no scaling, truncation or intermediate sum, so its unsigned addition does not wrap exactly when the result is at least the base. The pass adds only nuw, runs only for 65816 subtargets, and leaves other GEPs to their existing flags and known bits. -mos-recover-near-nowrap=false disables it; the pass itself is named mos-near-nowrap-recovery, because opt accepts every legacy pass name as an option.

Implementation: OpenAI Codex CLI 0.157.1 (codex-tui), model gpt-6-astra, high reasoning effort; session 01a0e315-89ed-7e70-b7dc-fcc2940366d9. Upstream extraction, header split and near-only tests: Claude Code 2.1.283, model Claude Opus 5.5 (claude-opus-5-5), xhigh reasoning effort; session f79adc39-72b4-4dc5-abc1-849c14c5ce96.

